1790603691.900	**A. Woodblock Printing (Xylography):**
1790603691.900	The most ancient form of mechanical reproduction is woodblock printing. In China, this technique flourished as early as the Tang Dynasty (7th–10th centuries CE). Artisans would carve an entire page—text and image combined—into a single block of wood. This block was then inked and pressed onto paper or fabric. Early examples, such as Buddhist texts from the 8th century, demonstrate sophisticated artistry within this medium.
1790603691.900	
1790603691.900	Woodblock printing was highly effective for religious iconography and popular literature in East Asia because it allowed for relatively rapid reproduction of standardized images. However, its limitations were significant: each page required a unique block carving, making customization difficult and production slow when compared to later methods. Furthermore, the process was inherently static; if an error needed correction, the entire block had to be recarved.
1790603691.900	
